```java
public class CreateOrderRequest {

    @NotBlank(message = "Customer name is required")
    private String customerName;

    @Email(message = "A valid email address is required")
    private String customerEmail;

    @NotEmpty(message = "Order must contain at least one item")
    private List<@NotBlank String> items;

    @Min(value = 1, message = "Quantity must be at least 1")
    private int quantity;
}
```

`@NotNull`, `@NotEmpty`, and `@NotBlank` sound similar but check progressively stricter conditions, and are easy to mix up. `@NotNull` only rejects a `null` reference; an empty string `""` passes. `@NotEmpty` additionally requires a collection or string to have a size/length greater than zero: `null` fails, and so does `""`, but `"   "` (all whitespace) passes, because it technically has a non-zero length. `@NotBlank`, applicable to strings specifically, is the strictest of the three: it requires a trimmed length greater than zero, so `null`, `""`, and `"   "` all fail. For a required name field, use `@NotBlank`; use `@NotEmpty` for a required collection like `items`. Most other constraints, `@Email` and `@Size` included, treat `null` as valid, so an optional-but-well-formed field needs only `@Email`, while a required one needs `@NotBlank` as well (as here it would if `customerEmail` were mandatory).

An `@ExceptionHandler(SomeException.class)` method, declared inside a controller class, catches any exception of that type (or a subtype) thrown by *any handler method within that same controller*, and its return value becomes the response, typically wrapped in a `ResponseEntity` so you can set the HTTP status. Declaring it inside a controller works, but it only covers that one controller.

`@ControllerAdvice` (or `@RestControllerAdvice`, which is `@ControllerAdvice` plus `@ResponseBody`, like `@RestController` is to `@Controller`) applied to its own, separate class makes every `@ExceptionHandler` method inside it apply *globally*: across every controller in the entire application. This is the standard place to centralize error handling, rather than duplicating near-identical handler methods inside every controller:

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
public class ApiExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        Map<String, String> fieldErrors = new HashMap<>();
        for (FieldError error : ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors()) {
            fieldErrors.put(error.getField(), error.getDefaultMessage());
        }
        Map<String, Object> body = new HashMap<>();
        body.put("status", HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST.value());
        body.put("errors", fieldErrors);
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(body);
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(OrderNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> handleNotFound(OrderNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(Map.of("status", 404, "message", ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> handleGeneric(Exception ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
                .body(Map.of("status", 500, "message", "An unexpected error occurred"));
    }
}
```

One class now governs error responses for the application: field-level detail for validation failures, a domain-specific 404, and a vague catch-all. The catch-all is vague on purpose, so internal details don't leak to a client; log the exception inside that method or you will have nothing to debug with. It has a side effect to plan for: `Exception` also matches Spring MVC's own exceptions (unsupported method, unsupported media type, unreadable body), so those 405, 415 and 400 responses become 500s. Extending `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` keeps their proper statuses.

